Wait, actually, let me reconsider whether builder methods should return `this` (mutable) or a new instance. The `Match` type has each method returning a new `Match<...>` object (structurally new, with updated type parameters). For `MatchEach`, the return type is `MatchEach<i, o, [...handledCases, excluded], ...>` — the runtime object just needs to satisfy that shape. Returning `this` is fine. I'll return `this`.

Hmm, one consideration: immutability. If someone holds onto a reference to an earlier builder stage and later calls `.run()` on it after more `.with()` calls... with a mutable `this`, the earlier reference would also see the new clauses. Does this matter? For `match` (which is immutable), the old reference would short-circuit on its own state. For matchEach with mutable state, the old reference would evaluate the "later" clauses. This edge case is unlikely to be tested. But to be safe, I could make it immutable by copying the items array on each `.with()`/`.when()`/`.tap()`. The cost is cheap: `const next = new MatchEachExpression(this.input); next.items = [...this.items, newItem]; return next;`. This matches `match`'s immutability semantics and is safer. I'll go with immutable-style (new instance per clause). Actually, the items array is small; copying is fine.

Hmm wait, but there's a subtlety with evaluation order and "declaration order" — the items array's order is the declaration order. Copying preserves order. Good.

